body:
`is_class` is used to ask `Object` subtypes if they derive from a certain class.
It currently uses `String` parameters, which is wasteful because the string often needs to be allocate, and because it needs to do a lot of string comparisons.

We already have a much faster way of determining subtypes: `is_class_ptr`. It is annoying to use though, so I created a safe wrapper function from my implementation in https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/103708.
The new function is named `is_class` to encourage developers to use it.
The old function is renamed `is_class_by_name` to discourage developers to use it unless needed.

Changing callers of the function could be automated (search `is_class\("(\w+)")`, replace with `is_class<$1>()`).
~50 of the ~150 uses started erroring because of unknown types. I replaced those manually back to `is_class_by_name`, to avoid new includes. Future implementers may choose to add an include to change `is_class_by_name` usage to `is_class`.

## Benchmark
I determined that `is_class` can be 35x faster than `is_class_by_name` (formerly `is_class`):
```c++
	Node3D *node = memnew(Node3D);

	{
		auto t0 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
		for (int i = 0; i < 50000000; i++) {
			// Test
			node->is_class<Node2D>();
		}
		auto t1 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
		std::cout << std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::milliseconds>(t1 - t0).count() << "ms\n";
	}
	{
		auto t0 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
		for (int i = 0; i < 50000000; i++) {
			// Test
			node->is_class_by_name("Node2D");
		}
		auto t1 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
		std::cout << std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::milliseconds>(t1 - t0).count() << "ms\n";
	}
```

The test probably unrealistically favours `is_class_by_name` because memory is in cache during the test, so the practical difference may be even better.

## Notes on compatibility
This breaks compatibility to existing modules, which will have to choose either the new `is_class` or the old `is_class_by_name` manually. This will be a static failure, so no risks are involved.
It does not break binary compatibility.
